WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.801
-Our next guest is
a very funny stand-up comedian

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.303
whose book "Misfit: Growing Up
Awkward in the '80s,"

00:00:04.303 --> 00:00:05.538
is available now,

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:07.206
and his latest special,
"Born on Third Base,"

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:09.709
premieres on Max December 21st.
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:11.844
-I don't mean to
distance myself from you,

00:00:11.844 --> 00:00:13.847
but I fly Comfort Plus.

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:17.950
[ Laughter ]

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:19.986
It's just -- it's a trade-off.

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:26.059
For an extra $135, usually,
I can get the leg room

00:00:26.059 --> 00:00:29.195
that prevents me
from having my legs amputated

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:31.331
because of blood clots.

00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:34.834
I cannot sit
in the coach class.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:36.135
It just doesn't work for me.

00:00:36.135 --> 00:00:39.138
So I'm going to
my Comfort Plus seat.

00:00:39.138 --> 00:00:43.410
I get so anxious
and embittered.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.112
I always say, "Honey, don't be
impressed with these people.

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:47.280
If they had
their [bleep] together,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.982
they'd be in a private jet
right now."

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:51.483
[ Laughter ]

00:00:51.483 --> 00:00:54.120
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Gary Gulman!

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.122
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:56.122 --> 00:01:09.134 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:09.134 --> 00:01:10.970
Welcome back!
-Oh. It's so --

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.072
I'm so excited to be back.
Thank you for having me on.

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:15.508
-I am -- always so lovely
to talk to you.

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.677
You always seem to be
in the best mood.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:20.580
And what I've appreciated,
though, is you have been so open

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:25.284
and honest about
mental-health issues, recovery.

00:01:25.284 --> 00:01:27.420
You did a great special
called "The Great Depresh."

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.657
Have you -- have you
enjoyed the feedback from people

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:32.859
who appreciate how much you talk
about those issues?

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:34.861
-Oh, my word.
It came out four years ago,

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:38.597
and yet I get e-mails every day
from somebody who it helped,

00:01:38.597 --> 00:01:39.966
and they always thank me.

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:41.834
And I'm like, "No,
you did all the hard work.

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:44.303
I just made a special that made
you feel better for an hour."

00:01:44.303 --> 00:01:46.172
So I'm just so grateful.

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:49.742
I felt obligated, after I felt
better, to share this story

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.177
and give some people hope.

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:53.612
So I'm so grateful
at how it's turned out.

00:01:53.612 --> 00:01:55.681
-And now "Misfit."
-Yes.

00:01:55.681 --> 00:01:57.082
-This is a book.
How did you come up

00:01:57.082 --> 00:01:59.585
with the title for "Misfit"
about your growing-up years?

00:01:59.585 --> 00:02:01.954
-Well, I'm a big Rush fan.
-Okay.

00:02:01.954 --> 00:02:03.823
-And so they had this song

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:05.725
that brought me great comfort
in the '80s,

00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:08.461
in my adolescence,
was called "Subdivisions."

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:10.729
And there was a line in there
where they said --

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:12.965
It's about the suburbs.
It says, "Nowhere else

00:02:12.965 --> 00:02:16.302
is the dreamer
or the misfit so alone."

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:19.572
And so I wanted --
when I decided to write a book,

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:21.407
I said, "I want
to make these misfits,

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:23.209
these people
who felt uncomfortable

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:24.510
in high school and older --

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.713
and we still don't fit in
as adults sometimes --

00:02:27.713 --> 00:02:29.815
that they would feel
a little bit better about that.

00:02:29.815 --> 00:02:31.517
So I don't sing
or play the drums,

00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:34.187
but I can write funny,
funny stories, so...

00:02:34.187 --> 00:02:36.289
-I'm so impressed
with your recall

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:38.858
of the things you experienced
at that young age.

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:40.727
Did it all come
flooding back to you,

00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:41.927
like, your time in the '80s,

00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:44.163
or did you have to,
like, dig to find them?

00:02:44.163 --> 00:02:45.731
-No, it didn't have
to flood back

00:02:45.731 --> 00:02:48.501
because these were stories
that had haunted me

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:51.270
since they happened
in a lot of cases.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:54.206
Or, I've told this --
-The tight focus of trauma.

00:02:54.206 --> 00:02:56.241
Yeah.
-You never forget being

00:02:56.241 --> 00:03:00.346
in first grade, and it's the --
it was in September.

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.882
And I said
to my first grade teacher,

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.284
who was also
my next-door neighbor,

00:03:04.284 --> 00:03:05.384
which was a thing back then,

00:03:05.384 --> 00:03:07.219
you would just live
right next door.

00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:09.622
And so it was Mrs. Burns,
and I said,

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:11.824
"Who am I going to have
for second grade, Mrs. Burns?"

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:13.860
And she said, "Your father
hasn't told you?"

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.763
And I was repeating
the first grade.

00:03:16.763 --> 00:03:18.364
Even though I was in Sun Up,

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:22.001
which was the top reading group,
it was the top reading group,

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:25.838
and the Sun Up kids
read gorgeous, beautifully.

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:27.907
No fingers
or anything like that.

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:29.708
[ Laughter ]
And then I found out --

00:03:29.708 --> 00:03:31.143
So you never forget that.

00:03:31.143 --> 00:03:33.145
-This is not your first book.
I was very impressed.

00:03:33.145 --> 00:03:34.980
You wrote this when you were
in second grade?

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:37.349
-Yeah. This was my first book.
-"The Lonely Tree."

00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:38.851
-"The Lonely Tree."
The working --

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:41.020
-Now, I will say, I've got
a second grader right now,

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.288
and I saw this,
I'm like, "Oh, my God.

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:43.789
This is so much better than what
my son could do."

00:03:43.789 --> 00:03:45.491
-[ Laughing ] Really?
-So now I'm happy to know

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:47.527
you did first grade twice.
-Yes. Yeah!

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:48.528
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.528 --> 00:03:50.462
-I know he's got
a full nother year.

00:03:50.462 --> 00:03:51.797
-That's so good.
Yeah.

00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:53.966
-So, "The Lonely Tree."
-I should've been in third.

00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:56.469
-Yeah. This is --
But this is very well done.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:57.737
-Oh, thank you.
Thank you.

00:03:57.737 --> 00:04:00.973
The working title
was "A Cry for Help."

00:04:00.973 --> 00:04:02.841
-"A Cry for Help," yeah.
-Yes.

00:04:02.841 --> 00:04:04.277
-When you have a kid
who writes a book called

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:06.712
"The Lonely Tree"...
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.712 --> 00:04:09.515
...they're like,
"Let's red-flag this one."

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:11.050
But it's really beautiful.
-Oh, thank you.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.185
-And look at this.
Like, I like that it's --

00:04:13.185 --> 00:04:17.656
we're gonna get all love --
love, Gary, to your readers.

00:04:17.656 --> 00:04:19.925
-Oh, yeah.
[ Laughing ]

00:04:19.925 --> 00:04:23.363
-Well, in those days,
I didn't have a lot of income,

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:26.065
so I would give gifts
I made from school.

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:28.400
And that was a present
to my mom and dad,

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.169
who didn't even live
in the same home.

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:34.040
So my mom wound up keeping it,
but my dad brought it around

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:36.142
and tried to get it published,
which is insane.

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:38.211
-Aww, that's very sweet.
-It is very sweet.

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:39.512
-That's very sweet.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:41.480
-Did you -- speaking of,
you grew up with

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.484
not a lot of money, and
you actually talked about that

00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:46.252
in "Born on Third Base."
-Yes.

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:48.387
-And you talk about
income inequality,

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:49.856
and it's something
you're still noticing.

00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:52.391
-Yeah. It wasn't
as bad when we grew up.

00:04:52.391 --> 00:04:55.294
Now, and even in our industry,
for instance,

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:59.598
do you remember the guy
who played Jerry on "Seinfeld"?

00:04:59.598 --> 00:05:00.833
-Yeah, yeah.
-Okay.

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:05.037
[ Laughter ]

00:05:05.037 --> 00:05:09.308
He is worth $1 billion.
-What?

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:14.514
-Yeah, $1 billion.
He has a building in Manhattan

00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:17.884
where he houses an extensive
Porsche collection.

00:05:17.884 --> 00:05:21.621
And I just --
I hate to compare, but...

00:05:21.621 --> 00:05:22.889
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:24.357
...my wife and I,
we don't have enough room

00:05:24.357 --> 00:05:26.826
on our kitchen counter
to keep the toaster

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:29.594
on display at all times.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.594 --> 00:05:31.431
We have to keep it --

00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:33.166
I said, "Let's keep it
in a cabinet,"

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:34.967
and then she said,
"You mean the cabinet?"

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:35.968
-Yeah, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:37.603
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:38.938
-And then when you have
guests over and you're like,

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:41.207
"Do you want toast?"
and it's like this thing.

00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:42.508
-The beauty of this audience is,

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:44.744
you're all picturing
a four-slicer.

00:05:44.744 --> 00:05:47.212
[ Laughter ]

00:05:47.212 --> 00:05:49.715
It's -- It's the deuce.
-Yeah.

00:05:49.715 --> 00:05:51.350
Oh, it's only two slices?
-Yes!

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:52.985
-You don't even have room
on a counter

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:54.887
for the double-slicer.
-No, because we have to keep

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:56.455
the coffee maker.
-Well, of course.

00:05:56.455 --> 00:05:58.824
-Because that's the only thing
that makes life tolerable.

00:05:58.824 --> 00:06:00.626
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:00.626 --> 00:06:02.428
-But you were obviously --
All jokes aside,

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:03.629
you're doing very well.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:04.896
You're a very successful
comedian.

00:06:04.896 --> 00:06:06.466
-Oh, thank you.
-And you have

00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:08.467
recently upgraded your wardrobe.

00:06:08.467 --> 00:06:10.603
-Oh, yes.
Um, I don't want you

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:12.538
to feel sorry for me
about the countertop,

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:16.275
because this is
all Banana Republic.

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:17.609
-Wow!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:17.609 --> 00:06:20.012
-Yeah, I'm in the --

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:23.282
I'm in the
Banana Republic tax bracket.

00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:26.318
Now, full disclosure,
I purchased this

00:06:26.318 --> 00:06:29.555
during one of Banana Republic's
thrice weekly 40% off sales.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:30.689
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.689 --> 00:06:32.491
-If you put any effort in,

00:06:32.491 --> 00:06:35.694
you can wear this ensemble
for the price of Old Navy.

00:06:35.694 --> 00:06:37.629
You just --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:40.432
You do -- You wait
for the 40% off sale,

00:06:40.432 --> 00:06:43.236
which is, like, now, and then...
[ Laughter ]

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:45.705
...and then you go online,
you get the 20% off promo code,

00:06:45.705 --> 00:06:48.708
and I'll give you my code.
It's "Winter savings."

00:06:48.708 --> 00:06:50.042
[ Laughter ]
"Winter savings."

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:51.610
-Are you allowed --
Are they cool with you

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:54.946
just giving that to everyone?
-But here's the twist.

00:06:54.946 --> 00:06:57.349
I always think to myself,
"Oh, I could've guessed that,"

00:06:57.349 --> 00:06:59.185
when I get the promo code.

00:06:59.185 --> 00:07:01.421
But the truth is,
I couldn't have guessed it.

00:07:01.421 --> 00:07:04.089
There's so --
It's just a little...

00:07:04.089 --> 00:07:06.792
The "savings" is spelled
with a dollar sign.

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:08.994
-Ah.
-Oh. Banana...

00:07:08.994 --> 00:07:11.964
-That's how they tell the
riffraff from the insiders.

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:15.635
-$30 for the cardigan.
How much for the whimsy?

00:07:15.635 --> 00:07:17.102
[ Laughter ]

00:07:17.102 --> 00:07:20.740
Free whimsy at Banana Republic,
while supplies last.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:22.675
-Yeah.
Now I have one --

00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:24.844
I know you have loved comedy
from a young age.

00:07:24.844 --> 00:07:26.846
-Oh, yeah.
-But from a young age,

00:07:26.846 --> 00:07:28.280
there was a piece of comedy...
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:30.449
-...that you did not care for,
which is crowd work.

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:31.918
-Yes.
-Okay. What was it about

00:07:31.918 --> 00:07:33.051
crowd work that you didn't like?

00:07:33.051 --> 00:07:34.153
-Well, in high school,
we would go

00:07:34.153 --> 00:07:36.188
to Nick's Comedy Stop in Boston.

00:07:36.188 --> 00:07:38.624
-Yes.
-And I wanted to hear the jokes

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:41.860
and then tell them to kids
in school on Monday.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:44.129
-Totally understand.
-And you can't do that if it's,

00:07:44.129 --> 00:07:46.966
"Hey, what do you do for work?"
-Right.

00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:48.400
-And I just remember thinking,

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.236
"All right, if anybody asks me
what I did for work..."

00:07:51.236 --> 00:07:55.007
To this day, if I was
at a comedy show, incognito...

00:07:55.007 --> 00:07:56.742
[ Laughs ]

00:07:56.742 --> 00:07:58.010
...and they said,
"What do you do for work?"

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:01.447
I would stand up and say,
"What do you do for work?"

00:08:01.447 --> 00:08:02.648
[ Laughter ]

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:04.283
"I -- I didn't come here

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:07.486
to be forcibly cast
in your TikTok video."

00:08:07.486 --> 00:08:09.021
[ Laughter ]

00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:12.625
"I want to tell these jokes
at school on Monday."

00:08:12.625 --> 00:08:14.226
[ Laughter ]

00:08:14.226 --> 00:08:16.329
-You would still do that today.
-[ Laughs ]

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:18.364
-And you don't think that last
part would be weird to them?

00:08:18.364 --> 00:08:20.199
-That would be very odd, yes.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:20.199 --> 00:08:21.233
-They're like, what?

00:08:21.233 --> 00:08:23.369
How many times
have you been held back?

00:08:23.369 --> 00:08:24.537
[ Laughter ]

00:08:24.537 --> 00:08:26.072
This book is so wonderful.
-Oh, thank you.

00:08:26.072 --> 00:08:27.807
-It really is a beautiful --
-And you wrote the blurb

00:08:27.807 --> 00:08:28.974
on the back.
-I blurbed it.

00:08:28.974 --> 00:08:30.643
I like to blurb it.
-Yeah.

00:08:30.643 --> 00:08:32.979
-Look, it was
an honor to blurb your work.

00:08:32.979 --> 00:08:34.046
-It's an honor to have
your name on there,

00:08:34.046 --> 00:08:35.815
because I've admired you
from the get-go.

00:08:35.815 --> 00:08:37.282
Meaning from "Jump Street."

00:08:37.282 --> 00:08:38.784
-Oh, wow.
Those are --

00:08:38.784 --> 00:08:40.719
Yeah. I think almost
everybody uses those the same.

00:08:40.719 --> 00:08:42.221
-Best lyrics.
[ Both laugh ]

00:08:42.221 --> 00:08:43.856
-It's such a pleasure
to see you.

00:08:43.856 --> 00:08:45.124
Guys, this is
the great Gary Gulman.

00:08:45.124 --> 00:08:46.492
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:46.492 --> 00:08:48.594
-"Born on Third Base" premieres
on Max December 21st.

00:08:48.594 --> 00:08:50.730
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

